meta { title = Arrays and maps, order = 4, group = Learn }

Arrays use square brackets with space-separated elements. Read and write an element with an index in square brackets. Indexes start at zero.

h2 { Transform an array }

The mapped pipe -> * calls a function for each element. Here it produces [2 4 6], then the indexed assignment replaces the middle element with 42.

{{example:collections}}

h2 { Ordinary mapping and parallel mapping }

This example uses ordinary local calls. Mapping alone does not start workers. The parallel lesson adds a worker target and awaits the mapped results.

Kalandra maps can pass an index and the original collection as extra callback arguments. That matters for checked functions and for the types crossing a worker boundary.

Ranges are also supported, but direct parallel range maps are rejected. Use an array for the parallel examples. Collection membership and bulk range assignment are not covered by this guide; their semantics are not supplied by the port.

